%% posix-on-wasm.mgp - POSIX on WebAssembly (or: what is an OS anyway?)
%% A gucOS talk (todos/0221): the list of things operating systems do and
%% how each applies to gucOS - DO / DON'T / DON'T NEED TO. Uses only
%% directives the 0119 port renders (the 0202 whitelist).
%deffont "standard" tfont "/usr/share/fonts/mono.ttf"
%default 1 area 90 90, size 2, fore "white", back "#301848", font "standard"
%default 2 size 9, vgap 20, prefix " ", center
%default 3 size 5, fore "yellow", vgap 30
%tab 1 size 4, vgap 40, prefix "  ", icon box "SpringGreen" 50, left
%tab 2 size 3, vgap 40, prefix "      ", icon arc "gold" 50, left
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

POSIX on WebAssembly

or: what is an OS anyway?

%center
%size 3, fore "gray70"
space / click: next page    b: back    q: quit
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

What is an OS anyway?

	"a thing that talks to the hardware
	directly on behalf of host programs"
		that is just ONE of the many things
		an operating system does
	this talk: the list of things an OS does,
	and how each one applies to gucOS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

What is POSIX?

	basically an operating system in the browser
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

Isn't emscripten POSIX?

	yes and no
	emscripten lets you compile many programs
	that expect a posix environment and
	run them in the browser
		but it doesn't give you the process
		management features of an OS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

But no desktop for free

	so it doesn't exactly give you a desktop
	or window manager where you can click on
	apps and have them all just run in
	parallel and stuff, for free
	you could build on top of it to do that
		but that is actual real work
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

What makes gucOS different?

	so what is it, and what does it do?
	(and don't operating systems normally
	run on the hardware directly?)
		sort the OS job list three ways:
		DO / DON'T / DON'T NEED TO
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

gucOS: DO

	process management
	sound server
		mixes audio from multiple processes
	desktop compositor and window manager
	persistent file system - blockfs
	real shell - busybox hush
	address space isolation
		(but see the MMU slide)
	signals - cooperative
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

gucOS: DON'T

	threads - pthreads etc
		tricky with shared memory,
		adds overhead
	atomics
	fork / exec
		also doesn't work well with wasm
	preemptive signals
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

gucOS: DON'T NEED TO

	SMP - multicore handled by the browser
	low-level drivers - just web APIs
		the browser is the HAL
		(hardware abstraction layer)
	preemptive scheduler
		doesn't work well with wasm / web
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

DON'T NEED TO, part 2

	raw-disk-friendly file systems
		ext4 / ntfs are built for spinning
		disks and ssds
	MMU - direct hardware for memory management
	boot - bootloader / BIOS / UEFI
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

Why not just emulate?

	emulators like tinyemu or v86 CAN have
	preemptive threads
		you can run custom code after
		every cpu cycle
	but that's insanely slow
		the trade-off of emulation
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

Prior art

	Browsix - similar architecture, has fork,
		but no window manager
	Wasix - ambition for full POSIX on wasm,
		but pays a lot for it
	emulators - tinyemu, v86
	JIT emulators - WebVM / CheerpX
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%page

The end

%size 5, center
gucOS: POSIX enough, in a browser tab
%size 3, fore "gray70", center

this deck is plain text - right-click it,
pick Edit, and make it yours    (q quits)
